{"seeAlsoSections":[{"title":"Related Documentation","anchor":"Related-Documentation","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView"]},{"generated":true,"title":"Essentials","anchor":"Essentials","identifiers":["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/SwiftUIAndUIKitEquivalents"]}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates"]]},"metadata":{"role":"article","roleHeading":"Article","title":"Recipes","modules":[{"name":"ScreenStates"}]},"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Recipes","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"kind":"article","variants":[{"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/recipes"],"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}]}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"anchor":"Overview","type":"heading","text":"Overview"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"A single ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" models one screen. Real apps have several screens at once — a list next to a detail, one per tab, a sidebar next to its content — each of which typically wants its ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"own","type":"text"}]},{"text":" store, scoped to exactly what that screen shows. This article walks through three common shapes for that.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"anchor":"List-+-Detail","type":"heading","text":"List + Detail"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Give the list screen and the detail screen their own store, each scoped to what that screen actually needs — a ","type":"text"},{"code":"[Article]","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" summary for the list, a full ","type":"text"},{"code":"Article","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for the detail — rather than trying to stretch one store to cover both:","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["struct ArticlesListScreen: View {","    @State private var store = ScreenStateStore<[Article]>()","","    var body: some View {","        NavigationStack {","            ScreenStateView(store.state, onRetry: reload) { articles in","                List(articles) { article in","                    NavigationLink(article.title, value: article.id)","                }","            }","            .navigationDestination(for: Article.ID.self) { id in","                ArticleDetailScreen(articleID: id)","            }","            .task { await reload() }","        }","    }","","    private func reload() async {","        await store.loadCollection { try await api.fetchArticles() }","    }","}","","struct ArticleDetailScreen: View {","    let articleID: Article.ID","    @State private var store = ScreenStateStore<Article>()","","    var body: some View {","        ScreenStateView(store.state, onRetry: reload) { article in","            ArticleDetailView(article: article)","        }","        .task(id: articleID) { await reload() }","    }","","    private func reload() async {","        await store.load { try await api.fetchArticle(id: articleID) }","    }","}"]},{"inlineContent":[{"code":"navigationDestination(for:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" creates a fresh ","type":"text"},{"code":"ArticleDetailScreen","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — and so a fresh store, starting at the default ","type":"text"},{"code":".loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" — every time a row is tapped, so there’s no manual reset to write. ","type":"text"},{"code":".task(id: articleID)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (rather than a plain ","type":"text"},{"code":".task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":") is what makes this reusable in the next recipe below, where the same screen’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"articleID","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" can change ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"without","type":"text"}]},{"text":" the view itself being recreated.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":2,"anchor":"One-Store-per-Tab","type":"heading","text":"One Store per Tab"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Give each tab’s screen its own ","type":"text"},{"code":"@State","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" store. "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"TabView"},{"type":"text","text":" preserves each tab’s view identity across switches, so the store — and the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".task"},{"type":"text","text":" that loads it — is created once and lives for as long as the tab does, not recreated every time the user switches back to it:"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"syntax":"swift","code":["struct RootTabView: View {","    var body: some View {","        TabView {","            ArticlesTab()","                .tabItem { Label(\"Articles\", systemImage: \"newspaper\") }","            ProfileTab()","                .tabItem { Label(\"Profile\", systemImage: \"person\") }","        }","    }","}","","struct ArticlesTab: View {","    @State private var store = ScreenStateStore<[Article]>()","","    var body: some View {","        NavigationStack {","            ScreenStateView(store.state, onRetry: reload) { articles in","                List(articles) { Text($0.title) }","            }","            .task { await reload() }","        }","    }","","    private func reload() async {","        await store.loadCollection { try await api.fetchArticles() }","    }","}"],"type":"codeListing"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Switching away from and back to a tab shows whatever ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"store.state"},{"type":"text","text":" already holds — no flash back to "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".loading"},{"text":". If the data can go stale while another tab is active, wire up ","type":"text"},{"code":"refreshCollection(_:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" (see ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"},{"type":"text","text":") instead of relying on the initial "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".task"},{"text":" alone.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"anchor":"Master-Detail-with-NavigationSplitView","type":"heading","text":"Master-Detail with NavigationSplitView"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"code":"NavigationSplitView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" shows the sidebar and detail columns side by side, so — unlike the pushed detail screen above — selecting a different row updates the ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"same","type":"text"}]},{"text":" detail view instance’s ","type":"text"},{"code":"articleID","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" rather than creating a new one. This is exactly why the detail screen from ","type":"text"},{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"text":"List + Detail","type":"text"}]},{"text":" used ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".task(id: articleID)"},{"text":": it cancels and restarts the load whenever ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"articleID"},{"text":" changes, which a plain ","type":"text"},{"code":".task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" would miss.","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["struct ArticlesSplitView: View {","    @State private var listStore = ScreenStateStore<[Article]>()","    @State private var selection: Article.ID?","","    var body: some View {","        NavigationSplitView {","            ScreenStateView(listStore.state) { articles in","                List(articles, selection: $selection) { article in","                    Text(article.title).tag(article.id)","                }","            }","            .task { await listStore.loadCollection { try await api.fetchArticles() } }","        } detail: {","            if let selection {","                ArticleDetailScreen(articleID: selection) \/\/ reused as-is from List + Detail","            } else {","                ContentUnavailableView(\"Select an Article\", systemImage: \"newspaper\")","            }","        }","    }","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The sidebar list and the detail content are still two independent stores, each following the same “one store, scoped to one screen” shape as the other two recipes — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"NavigationSplitView"},{"text":" only changes how they’re laid out, not how they’re driven.","type":"text"}]}],"kind":"content"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Common multi-screen patterns built on "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"references":{"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates":{"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Give any screen a standard "},{"code":"Empty","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" \/ "},{"code":"Loading","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" \/ "},{"code":"Data","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" \/ "},{"code":"Error","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" state, usable from both UIKit and SwiftUI."}],"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates","role":"collection","title":"ScreenStates","type":"topic"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateStore":{"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatestore","abstract":[{"text":"An ","type":"text"},{"code":"@Observable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" holder of a screen’s ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":", driving both","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"SwiftUI and UIKit consumers from a single source of truth.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"class"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateStore"}],"title":"ScreenStateStore","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateStore"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateView":{"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Renders the right content for a "},{"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":", switching between"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Empty \/ Loading \/ Data \/ Error automatically."}],"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstateview","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateView"}],"title":"ScreenStateView","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateView"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/GettingStartedWithUIKit":{"kind":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/gettingstartedwithuikit","abstract":[{"text":"Drive a UIKit screen’s four states with ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateContainerView"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"role":"article","title":"Getting Started with UIKit","type":"topic"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI":{"type":"topic","role":"article","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI","title":"Getting Started with SwiftUI","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Drive a SwiftUI screen’s four states with "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateStore"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"reference","isActive":true,"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/gettingstartedwithswiftui","kind":"article"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenStateContainerView":{"kind":"symbol","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateContainerView","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstatecontainerview","abstract":[{"text":"A ","type":"text"},{"code":"UIView","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" that swaps its content for the right placeholder based on a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":", mirroring ","type":"text"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateView","isActive":true,"type":"reference"},{"text":" from the SwiftUI side.","type":"text"}],"role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"class"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateContainerView"}],"title":"ScreenStateContainerView","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenStateContainerView"}],"type":"topic"},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/ScreenState":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState","url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/screenstate","type":"topic","role":"symbol","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"enum"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenState"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The four canonical states of a screen: nothing to show yet, in flight,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"showing data, or failed."}],"title":"ScreenState","kind":"symbol","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"ScreenState"}]},"doc://screenstates.ScreenStates/documentation/ScreenStates/SwiftUIAndUIKitEquivalents":{"url":"\/documentation\/screenstates\/swiftuianduikitequivalents","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/SwiftUIAndUIKitEquivalents","type":"topic","role":"article","abstract":[{"text":"A single table matching every SwiftUI-side type to its UIKit counterpart (and calling out the handful with no equivalent at all), for a reader who already knows one paradigm and wants the other’s version of the same thing at a glance.","type":"text"}],"title":"SwiftUI & UIKit Equivalents","kind":"article"}}}